WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.020
Being a convincing actor is about more than
just crying on cue and perfecting your angry

00:00:05.020 --> 00:00:06.069
stares.

00:00:06.069 --> 00:00:10.059
Actors also need to be trained to speak with
foreign accents for many of their roles -- whether

00:00:10.059 --> 00:00:14.070
it's to conceal their own native tongue or
to convince audiences they hail from a whole

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:15.480
different homefront.

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:19.470
In the case of these Hollywood stars, their
dialect coaches must've left something to

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:24.270
be desired because their terrible on-screen
accents will probably never disappear from

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.040
their blooper reels.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.590
Gerard Butler

00:00:27.590 --> 00:00:32.290
He may have been born and raised in Scotland,
but Gerard Butler doesn't get to play Scottish

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:36.910
characters as often as he might like, which
means there are many examples of him attempting

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:39.910
to sound American and failing spectacularly.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:44.800
Perhaps no effort has been quite as tragic
as 2010's The Bounty Hunter, in which he has

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:48.720
the darndest time trying to make use of his
hundreds of dialect sessions.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.480
"And what I do is I hunt down criminals.

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.220
Idiots who jumped bail, specifically."

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.580
Oddly enough, his Irish accent in P.S.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.530
I Love You isn't much better either.

00:00:59.530 --> 00:01:06.650
"I would like to apologize to the nation of
Ireland for completely abusing your accent."

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:08.330
Keanu Reeves

00:01:08.330 --> 00:01:14.080
There's one thing Keanu Reeves can do better
than anyone in the entire acting business:

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.140
"WOAH."

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.480
But playing a Brit is just not in his skillset.

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:23.680
He proved as much when he starred in Bram
Stoker's Dracula and made everyone laugh for

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.000
all the wrong reasons with his faux English
affectation.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.710
"I've seen many strange things already.

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.729
Bloody wolves chasing me through some blue
inferno."

00:01:33.729 --> 00:01:37.729
Director Francis Ford Coppola later blamed
himself for the famously poor performance

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:40.590
in the 1992 drama, telling Entertainment Weekly:

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.030
"We knew that it was tough for him to affect
an English accent.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:45.760
He tried so hard.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:49.600
That was the problem, actually—he wanted
to do it perfectly and in trying to do it

00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:51.830
perfectly it came off as stilted."

00:01:51.830 --> 00:01:55.120
It definitely says something that he was able
to upstage that hairdo.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:56.440
"Yes sir.

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.060
I will give it my full attention."

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:00.770
John Malkovich

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:06.560
If you're going to put on a Russian accent,
at least try not to sound like a KGB cartoon.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:11.400
That's the lesson John Malkovich apparently
did not learn before he became a Soviet gangster

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:12.680
in Rounders.

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:17.680
The outrageous accent is not only extra phlegm-y,
but it borders on the insane.

00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:21.500
"Pay that man his money."

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:26.969
Apparently, even Malkovich knew he wasn't
delivering a quality depiction there because

00:02:26.969 --> 00:02:29.010
co-star Matt Damon later revealed …

00:02:29.010 --> 00:02:32.480
"And he looks at me and he goes, 'I'm a terrible
actor.'"

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:35.819
But, hey, at least we got this Damon-ism out
of the deal.

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:41.700
"If you don't have my money, then you are
mine."

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.400
Brad Pitt

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:49.749
An Irish accent is incredibly difficult to
fake, and Brad Pitt knows all about that.

00:02:49.749 --> 00:02:53.049
The actor trained with a dialect coach to
get his dialect down pat …

00:02:53.049 --> 00:02:57.029
"Accents have never been my strong point,
so, it was uh…

00:02:57.029 --> 00:02:58.949
I put a lot of work into it."

00:02:58.949 --> 00:03:04.000
But in The Devil's Own, the Hollywood heartthrob
failed to present that patented Irish lilt.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.079
"The meat looks delicious, what is it?"

00:03:08.079 --> 00:03:10.540
"It's… it's corned beef and cabbage.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:13.930
I thought that's all you ate over there."

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:18.740
Hey, at least he knows how to speak Italian
in a pinch.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:19.800
"Gratzie."

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:22.430
Kevin Costner

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.969
Robin Hood: Princes of Thieves wasn't exactly
a critical darling, and it hasn't really stood

00:03:26.969 --> 00:03:28.629
the test of time with audiences, either.

00:03:28.629 --> 00:03:34.329
But Kevin Costner's terrible take on a British
accent in the movie is still absolutely unforgettable.

00:03:34.329 --> 00:03:38.799
"If I must, I will take you a piece at a time."

00:03:38.799 --> 00:03:42.480
Hey, it might not have even been a British
accent for all we know because he sounds more

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:47.549
like a West Coast dude indulging in the Southern
California sun than a man rummaging through

00:03:47.549 --> 00:03:49.379
the forests of Nottingham.

00:03:49.379 --> 00:03:52.340
Except he's totally supposed to be English.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:56.870
"This is English courage."

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:57.989
Cameron Diaz

00:03:57.989 --> 00:04:03.849
Gangs of New York is a beautiful film anchored
by one heck of a performance from Daniel Day-Lewis,

00:04:03.849 --> 00:04:08.159
but there's no question that Cameron Diaz's
terrible accent tends to take you out of it.

00:04:08.159 --> 00:04:12.549
While there are moments in the 2002 film when
leading man Leonardo DiCaprio also seems to

00:04:12.549 --> 00:04:18.150
forget to flip his accent switch, Diaz rarely
turns hers on—and when she does, it's not

00:04:18.150 --> 00:04:19.150
good.

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:21.820
"Oh I ain't started working yet."

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:26.980
Not only does she not sound Irish, but she
doesn't sound like she's from 1863 either.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:30.650
"Maybe when you're aiming a little straighter."

00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.220
Angelina Jolie

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.790
There are a lot of terrible aspects of Oliver
Stone's Alexander, but Angelina Jolie's wacky

00:04:37.790 --> 00:04:41.130
accent is one of the most bizarre elements
of the whole movie.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.130
"Oh.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.050
Now who is exaggerating?"

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:48.320
To be fair to her, Colin Farrell decided to
keep his native Irish tongue while starring

00:04:48.320 --> 00:04:53.440
as the title Greek conqueror, and Val Kilmer
and Jared Leto also followed suit for some

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:54.440
strange reason.

00:04:54.440 --> 00:04:59.690
So, that left Jolie all by her lonesome, trying
to bring some Macedonian realness to her portrayal

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:04.940
of Olympia, but she sounds more like a Russian
mafiosa than the real deal.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:08.950
Jolie might've proven herself as having a
healthy dialect spread with her British tones

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:14.040
in Tomb Raider and her French-Cuban prose
in A Mighty Heart, but this is one flub that

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:15.840
won't soon be forgotten.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:17.930
"Forgive me."

00:05:17.930 --> 00:05:18.950
Thanks for watching!

00:05:18.950 --> 00:05:21.540
